Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero Btrfs backend: original native Zero implementation, bounded writable profile

fs/btrfs.zero is the same state machine used by the kernel and host tests.
It contains no C, Rust, assembly, imported filesystem code or external runtime.
The Zero compiler emits native code directly. External mkfs.btrfs, btrfs check
and btrfs restore are independent fixture/verification tools only.

Implemented operations
- Probe/validate, list the top-level root, read a file, create an empty file,
  create-or-replace file contents, remove a file.
- Case-sensitive names of 1..31 printable ASCII bytes, excluding slash,
  backslash, dot and dot-dot names. Each file is at most 400 bytes, inline.
- Persistent standard Btrfs inode items, inode references, hashed directory
  items, directory indices and inline extent data. Existing files written by
  mkfs.btrfs --rootdir are read and modified by this driver.
- Empty files have no data extent. Rewriting nonempty to empty and vice versa
  is supported. Deletion compacts the leaf and makes its capacity reusable.
- File bytes are not interpreted as a programming language or host pathname.

Explicit supported volume profile
- A whole filesystem or partition at the ABI-provided base LBA, on one device
  with internal device ID1. ATA LBA28 bound: disk address below 128GiB.
- At least64MiB+4096B; at most128GiB. Primary and64MiB mirror superblocks.
- CRC32C checksums;4096-byte sectors;16384-byte tree nodes.
- compat_flags=0, compat_ro_flags=0, incompat_flags=321 or833:
  MIXED_BACKREF, EXTENDED_IREF, SKINNY_METADATA, optional NO_HOLES.
- No log root, no free-space cache generation (cache_generation=UINT64_MAX),
  no free-space-tree, RAID, DUP, mixed block groups or multiple devices.
- Exactly one nonoverlapping SINGLE system chunk, metadata chunk and data
  chunk. The data chunk is empty because every supported file is inline.
- Root tree and every referenced tree root are level0 leaves; root/extent/FS
  trees are checksum-validated on every operation. FS tree5, directory256,
  and the default root directory must refer to the top-level FS tree.
- No subvolumes/snapshots, shared metadata references, received roots,
  read-only roots, quota tree, directories below root, hard links, symlinks,
  xattrs, ACLs, compression, encryption, sparse/external extents or data
  checksums. Every metadata extent belongs to exactly one known tree root.
- Root and extent leaf capacity is preserved by COW replacement. The file
  tree does not split: insertion that cannot fit returns capacity failure
  before publishing or writing a replacement file-tree block.

A reproducible disposable test volume can be made using:
  truncate -s 256M fixture.img
  mkfs.btrfs -f -m single -d single -R ^free-space-tree fixture.img
This is a test instruction, not an automatic reformat operation. No existing
user volume is reformatted or downgraded to make it fit the profile.
Default modern Btrfs volumes commonly use DUP and free-space-tree and are
therefore rejected by this initial backend. This is not full Btrfs support.

Transaction behavior
1. Validate superblock/profile, chunk ranges/UUIDs, all known metadata
   references and generation numbers, and the entire supported file tree.
   Check inode/ref/name/hash/index/data-length agreement and directory size.
2. Choose three free metadata blocks excluding active extents and superblock
   mirror stripes. Build a complete replacement file-tree leaf in RAM.
3. Write COW file-tree, root-tree and extent-tree leaves with CRC32C, updated
   root pointers/generations and replacement metadata backreferences.
   No active old leaf is overwritten. Space accounting remains unchanged.
4. Flush those leaves, publish and flush the primary superblock, then the
   secondary superblock. Both have checksums and a current root backup.
All operations reload metadata, so switching volumes needs no retained
backend state. No host filesystem mount is required.

Error semantics and limits
The common ABI reports success0, unsupported1, corrupt2, I/O3, missing4,
exists5, leaf/allocation capacity6, invalid argument7, output/file too large8,
recovery required9, wrong signature10. Error output length is zero. The kernel
may continue probing another filesystem only after wrong signature10.
A late I/O/flush failure can occur after the new primary root became visible;
the operation reports I/O failure and the next request reloads the volume.
This does not claim transactional rollback of a failed device write, hardware
power-loss guarantees, torn-sector recovery or generic filesystem repair.
The independent fault test proves consistency at eight simulated whole-write
or flush failure boundaries, not arbitrary physical torn writes.
Timestamps are preserved for existing files; newly created files have zero
(epoch/unknown) timestamps because this backend has no wall-clock service.
Directory ordering follows persistent Btrfs directory indices.

ABI and ownership
Entry400, helpers400..599; complete by loading H+72. H=memory+24576.
H fields follow fs/ABI.ztext (or the parent kernel's matching common contract).
Private state25600..28671, saved superblock28672..32767, two16KiB leaves at
32768/49152. No persistent or external allocation. Only declared volatile
scratch variables are used. Block I/O goes through state90 and includes H+80
partition LBA. io_write2 must perform a real device flush.

Evidence
verification/btrfs/test.sh, refusals.sh and mutants.sh exercise the same code.
results.txt: CRUD, empty transitions,31-byte names/400-byte payloads, directory
capacity/no mutation, delete/reuse, external btrfs check after every successful
matrix mutation,8 fail points with btrfs check and restore old-or-new data.
refusal-results.txt: bad checksum/foreign signature/truncated I/O, output
capacity, nonzero partition LBA and external check after partition mutation.
mutant-results.txt: checksummed orphan dir entry, mismatched inode ref,
generation overflow, wrapping chunk length, wrong metadata backref and inline
length mismatch; every rejection leaves the image SHA256 unchanged.
An externally populated image was read/updated and restored independently.
Both superblock mirror checksums were inspected with btrfs dump-super.

Format references, used as specifications rather than implementation code:
https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/dev/On-disk-format.html
Linux UAPI btrfs_tree.h on-disk record layouts and constants.
